---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const a11yCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Accessibility is a civil right with a spec', note: 'The web lesson\'s WCAG framework: perceivable, operable, understandable, robust (POUR) — legal baseline in many jurisdictions (the law lesson\'s compliance logic). ~1 in 6 people live with a disability: designing for the default user designs for a minority.' },
    { math: '2 · The mechanics', note: 'Semantic HTML (screen readers navigate structure — the web lesson\'s tags matter), alt text (convey function, not appearance), contrast ratios (4.5:1 for body text — the value lesson\'s numbers, inclusive), keyboard operability (every action reachable without a mouse), focus states (the design lesson\'s feedback, for navigation).' },
    { math: '3 · Design decisions are access decisions', note: 'Colour alone as signal fails colour-blind users (label AND colour); motion can sicken (respect reduced-motion); autoplay audio fights screen readers. Every pattern choice includes or excludes someone — unintentionally is still really.' },
    { math: '4 · Curb cuts: the universal payoff', note: 'Captions serve deaf users AND noisy commutes AND search indexing; keyboard access serves motor-impaired users AND power users AND test automation. Designing for the edges improves the middle — the documented pattern across every accessibility feature ever mainstreamed.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/design/intermediate/accessibility">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Accessibility is <strong>the design lesson\'s ethics made technical</strong>: a spec (WCAG),
    a law (equality acts) and a craft (inclusive patterns) — with the recurring surprise that
    building for the edges builds better for everyone.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="POUR, the mechanics, design as access, curb cuts" widget="equationStep" config={a11yCfg} />

  <h2>Practitioner essentials</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Test with real tools:</strong> screen readers (VoiceOver, NVDA), keyboard-only passes, automated linters (axe, Lighthouse) catching ~30–40% of issues — automation is the smoke alarm, human testing the fire brigade (the engineering lesson\'s testing tiers, inclusive edition).</li>
    <li><strong>Accessible ≠ ugly was always a myth:</strong> the constraint lesson\'s argument — strong type hierarchy and contrast are accessibility AND good typography; the alt-text discipline improves information design for everyone.</li>
    <li><strong>Plain language is accessibility:</strong> cognitive load counts as access: the journalism lesson\'s clarity discipline, the writing-craft lesson\'s audience awareness — jargon excludes invisibly.</li>
    <li><strong>Overlay widgets are the cautionary tale:</strong> bolt-on "accessibility" overlays fail the people they claim to serve (documented, litigated) — real accessibility is designed in, not pasted on; the profession\'s consensus statement is worth reading as an example of a field policing its own.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> WCAG 2.2 (W3C); WebAIM surveys; the curb-cut literature; the law lesson\'s compliance logic.</p>
</TopicPage>
